<Doc>
    <Code code={`bunx @abide/abide scaffold crm
cd crm
bun install
bun run dev`} />

    <p><code>bun run dev</code> runs <code>abide dev</code>, which serves the app on
        <code>localhost:3000</code> and restarts it on every save, with the open tab reloading behind
        it.</p>

    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>The scaffold writes</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>src/layout.abide</code></td><td>the root layout, around every page</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>src/index.abide</code></td><td>a page with a greeting and a click counter</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>src/getGreeting.handler.ts</code></td><td>the handler the page reads its greeting from</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>src/getGreeting.test.ts</code></td><td>a test that calls the handler</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>src/index.test.ts</code></td><td>a test that mounts the page, clicks it, and counts the work the click did</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>src/lib/app.css</code></td><td>the stylesheet the layout imports: Tailwind, built by the plugin <code>bunfig.toml</code> declares</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>CLAUDE.md</code> · <code>AGENTS.md</code></td><td>notes for a coding agent, importing the <code>ABIDE.md</code> the installed package holds: where its docs, as one markdown file, and its registry and rulebook are</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Dockerfile</code> · <code>.dockerignore</code></td><td>an image holding the compiled server and nothing else of the app. See <a href={url('/guide/test-measure-and-ship')}>Test, measure, ship</a></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>package.json</code></td><td>the scripts <code>dev</code>, <code>build</code>, <code>start</code>, <code>test</code>, <code>check</code>, <code>format</code>, <code>bench</code> and <code>routes</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>The CRM replaces the starter's page with a contacts page, and keeps the rest. Every card
        on this page offers the CRM as this chapter leaves it, at <code>/examples/crm-01.tar.gz</code>.</p>

    <Section title="A file's path is the address it answers">
        <p>A file's path below <code>src</code> is its address. Nothing else names a route.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Answers on</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>src/index.abide</code></td><td><code>/</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/contacts/index.abide</code></td><td><code>/contacts</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/contacts/listContacts.handler.ts</code></td><td><code>GET /contacts/listContacts</code>, the handler its default export declares</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/contacts/lib/ContactList.abide</code></td><td>no address: a component the pages import</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/contacts/lib/ContactList.test.ts</code></td><td>no address: what <code>bun test</code> runs</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Code that is not an address goes in a directory that never is, and a file's name
            decides where it runs:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>What goes in it</th><th>Where it runs</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>src/**</code></td><td>the app</td><td>both sides, unless its name says otherwise</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/**/</code></td><td>pages, layouts and handler files, routed by their path</td><td>a page on both sides; a handler on the server, with a call in its place in the browser</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>**/lib/**</code></td><td>components and modules</td><td>wherever they are imported</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>**/lib/**/&lt;name&gt;.server.&lt;extension&gt;</code></td><td>the database, secrets, anything the browser never loads</td><td>the server alone; a page importing it is a compile error</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>**/&lt;name&gt;.test.ts</code></td><td>tests, beside what they test</td><td><code>bun test</code>, never served</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>**/&lt;name&gt;.bench.ts</code></td><td>benches, beside what they time. See <a href={url('/guide/test-measure-and-ship')}>Test, measure, ship</a></td><td><code>bun run bench</code>, never served</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>**/&lt;NAME&gt;.param.ts</code></td><td>what a segment named <code>NAME</code> takes. See <a href={url('/guide/add-pages')}>Add pages</a></td><td>wherever an address is matched</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>**/&lt;name&gt;.d.ts</code></td><td>types, beside what they type</td><td>nowhere; the checker reads them</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>**/public/**</code></td><td>files answered as they are</td><td>nowhere; each is sent at its path</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Any other file beside the pages fails the build, so listing a directory lists its
            routes. The CRM keeps its contacts in <code>src/contacts/lib/CONTACTS.server.ts</code>, and
            only the handler reads it.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A page reads a handler like a value">
        <Example
            id="crm-01-contact-list"
            app="crm-01"
            title="`listContacts` — a handler file, read like a value"
            summary="The contacts page lists every contact. The list is empty until the handler answers, then fills in."
            files={{
                'src/contacts/lib/ContactList.abide': contactListSource,
                'src/contacts/listContacts.handler.ts': listContactsSource,
                'src/contacts/lib/ContactList.test.ts': contactListTestSource,
                'src/contacts/lib/ContactList.bench.ts': contactListBenchSource,
            }}
        >
            <ContactList />
        </Example>

        <p>The bench reads the list once the handler has answered, among 100 contacts and among
            1,000. Its ratio is what a read costs as the list grows: a read hands back what the
            handler answered, and copies nothing.</p>

        <p><code>listContacts.handler.ts</code> runs on the server. The list imports it like any module,
            and the browser is sent a call under the same name in its place, so there is no
            <code>fetch</code>, no route to register, and no second copy of the contact type.</p>

        <p>The test mounts the list, and the handler runs in process. A handler only one file
            calls can live in that file's <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> instead. See
            <a href={url('/guide/talk-to-the-server')}>Talk to the server</a>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The page is sent before the data lands">
        <Example
            id="crm-01-page-sent-first"
            app="crm-01"
            title="The whole contacts page, before the contacts answer"
            summary="The contacts handler takes 400 ms. Request the page the way a browser does, and compare when it is complete with when the contacts answer."
            files={{
                'src/contacts/lib/PageTiming.abide': pageTimingSource,
                'src/contacts/lib/PageTiming.test.ts': pageTimingTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <PageTiming />
        </Example>

        <p>Reading <code>contacts</code> starts the load, and the document does not wait for it.
            The page goes out as soon as it is built, with a hole where the list goes, and the hole
            fills when the contacts land. Neither file has a loading branch in it.</p>

        <p>The browser's call for the contacts is answered from what the server render already
            fetched, so the handler runs once. The cost is a client with no JavaScript: its hole
            stays empty.</p>

        <p>A page's <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> runs on the server to render it, then again in the
            browser. That is why the card reads <code>location</code> inside the click and not at the
            top of its script.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A value is read and written by name">
        <Example
            id="crm-01-star"
            app="crm-01"
            title="`starred` — read by name, written by name"
            summary="Grace Hopper is the contact you call most. Star her, then take the star off."
            files={{
                'src/contacts/lib/StarButton.abide': starButtonSource,
                'src/contacts/lib/StarButton.test.ts': starButtonTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <StarButton />
        </Example>

        <p>Naming <code>starred</code> in the markup reads it and follows it from then on.
            Assigning to it writes it and wakes everything that read it, with no re-render to ask
            for. <a href={url('/guide/edit-a-contact')}>Edit a contact</a> has every spelling of it.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Every value is a `Reactive`">
        <Example
            id="crm-01-one-type"
            app="crm-01"
            title="`Reactive` — four ways to get a value, one way to ask"
            summary="A note on a contact. Watch which rows say … on arrival, then type a note and save it."
            files={{
                'src/contacts/lib/ContactNote.abide': contactNoteSource,
                'src/contacts/lib/ContactNote.test.ts': contactNoteTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ContactNote />
        </Example>

        <p>A value the page owns, a value derived from it, a value loaded from a handler, and a
            value that waits for its first write: all four come from <code>reactive</code>, and all
            four are a <code>Reactive</code>. <code>ready()</code> asks each one the same question, so
            the branch you write for "not here yet" works on every one of them.</p>

        <p>Swapping a local value for a loaded one changes how it is declared and nothing that
            reads it.</p>

        <p>A <code>Reactive</code> can also be awaited, so code that takes a promise for a load asks
            <code>isReactive</code> first. A <code>Reactive</code> is a value, even while it loads.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The `abide` command">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does for the CRM</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>abide scaffold &lt;name&gt;</code></td><td>writes the starter app above</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide dev [--port &lt;n&gt;] [--hostname &lt;host&gt;]</code></td><td>serves the app and restarts it on every save; takes the first free port from 3000, or the one you name, on every interface or the one you name</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide run &lt;file&gt; [args…]</code></td><td>runs one script with the app's own modules, such as a script that loads contacts from a spreadsheet</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--help</code> · <code>-h</code> · <code>abide help</code></td><td>prints every command</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--version</code> · <code>-v</code></td><td>prints the version installed</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>abide dev</code> runs the same server <code>abide start</code> does, so a bug you
            see while you work is a bug you can reproduce in production. A stack trace names the
            <code>.abide</code> file and the line you wrote.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/guide/edit-a-contact')}>Edit a contact</a> — hold a contact the page
                can edit, and show what follows from it</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
